import * from os;
import * from notifications_win;

mod export notifications;

// DOCS -- public `notifications` module (UNIFIED_CORE_LIBRARY.md Phase 2).
// This is the unified, portable front of the Windows toast / tray-balloon
// notifications library. It contains ZERO native-bridge calls: the whole
// toast dispatch moved into the internal Windows backend
// std/notifications_win.sr, and the one entry that reaches it routes through
// the OS gate `if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS)` before calling it (osType()
// is std/os.sr's plain-Silver stand-in -- see that file for why the gate
// does not use the native os.type query).
//
// The else branch of the gate is unreachable on this backend (silverc only
// emits Windows PEs and osType() is the compile-time WINDOWS constant). For
// a future non-Windows backend the fallback follows the module's failure
// style: show() keeps printing the canonical parity line and returns
// without touching any backend.
//
// DISPATCH (per compiler, probed 2026-08 -- THIS IS THE BUILTIN-RESERVED
// NUANCE this split had to resolve; preserve exactly):
//
// - silverc-rs: `notifications` is NOT builtin-reserved
//   (src/main.rs:81 builtin_modules_set lists fs/json/yaml/env/url/hash/
//   path/bom/console/math/http only -- the word "notification" appears in
//   src/ only in comments and in the nativeToastShow bridge). Both the
//   wrapper (std/notifications.sr) and the backend (std/notifications_win.sr)
//   resolve as ORDINARY SOURCE modules from disk -- no rs source change is
//   needed or expected, verified by the full probe battery below.
//   (main.rs:207-210's comment about a test named notifications.sr only
//   covers the self-import shadowing case in resolve_imports.)
//
// - self-hosted silverc: `notifications` IS builtin-reserved in TWO places
//   (compiler/visibility.sr:610 visBuiltinModule and compiler_driver.sr
//   :120/:319/:395 findModulePath), so `import * from notifications;` is
//   NEVER collected from disk as an import. Instead compiler_driver.sr:753-
//   764 HAND-PREPENDS the text of std\notifications.sr into the aggregate
//   whenever the input contains the substring "from notifications" (the
//   same mechanism as exception/process/bom/llmai). So on the self-host the
//   wrapper is aggregated as PREPENDED SOURCE TEXT (its `mod export
//   notifications;` line is inert in a prepended aggregate; the definitions
//   simply become part of the single aggregate program), not as a resolved
//   module. The wrapper text must therefore stay single-parseable as a
//   fragment in either position -- it is (same posture as the exception/
//   process prepends).
//
// - The `notifications_win` backend is NOT builtin-reserved on the
//   self-host: the wrapper's `import * from notifications_win;` line
//   resolves it as an ordinary (internal) source module and collectImports
//   pulls it from disk. Same asymmetry as the smtp/http_server pairs (their
//   wrappers' `_win` imports also resolve normally; only the public names
//   are builtin-reserved on the self-host, and only when prepended --
//   smtp/http_server are not reserved at all).
//
// - silveri (context, no changes allowed in this split): `notifications` is
//   a true interpreter builtin (eval.sr:286 defines a notifications_module
//   global; main.sr:113 lists it as known). The interpreter never reads
//   std/notifications.sr; show() parity comes from builtins.sr:782. Untouched.
//
// PRE-SPLIT STATE (probed 2026-08 with fresh -o binaries on both compilers,
// see the verification section for the exact outputs): the old
// std/notifications.sr DID compile and run on both compilers -- unlike the
// ftp/smtp/http_server pre-splits, this module was LIVE: the NOTIFY: parity
// line printed byte-identically on silverc-rs and the self-host (and the
// rs-built binary additionally performed the real shell32 balloon; the
// self-host binary's nativeToastShow is a compiler-lowered no-op). show()
// already carried try/catch, so no failure path was observable. The ONLY
// thing this split changes is the INTERNAL routing of the native bridge
// (wrapper -> backend -> bridge) and the internal-visibility hardening of
// that backend; every observable behavior is preserved byte-identically
// (re-probed post-split, outputs cited in the module docs and report).
//
// API PRESERVATION (every public name of the pre-split file, unchanged):
// constants DEFAULT=0, SHORT=1, LONG=2, SILENT=1; class Notification with
// public fields title/body/icon/duration/sound/silent/group/tag/AppName
// (field ORDER preserved -- rs mis-indexes cross-module field reads past
// field 0, so these classes must stay defined IN the wrapper where user
// code bare-news them); class NotificationAction with public fields id/
// title/arguments; free function show(Notification) printing the canonical
// "NOTIFY: <title> | <body>" parity line before dispatching to the backend
// bridge. No renames were needed (`show`, `Notification`, `NotificationAction`,
// `DEFAULT`/`SHORT`/`LONG`/`SILENT` are not keywords on either compiler).
//
// ADDITIVE: none -- the pre-split surface was already exactly the intended
// API (the DOCS/247 action/lifecycle extras live only in the design doc,
// never in this module's code, and were not invented here).
//
// DEAD VS LIVE (post-split, per compiler):
// - silverc-rs: constants/classes/show parity line are LIVE (probed); the
//   backend bridge is LIVE and performs the real shell32 tray balloon
//   (fire-and-forget, auto-expires; probed once live -- harmless,
//   non-blocking, no dismissal required).
// - self-hosted silverc: constants/classes/show parity line are LIVE
//   (probed); the backend bridge is a compiler-lowered NO-OP by design
//   (mini_cg.sr:7044) -- the parity line is the entire observable behavior.
// - silveri: whole module is the interpreter builtin (NOTIFY: line via
//   builtins.sr:782); this file is never read.

public const int DEFAULT = 0;
public const int SHORT = 1;
public const int LONG = 2;
public const int SILENT = 1;

class Notification {
    string title;
    string body;
    string icon;
    int duration;
    int sound;
    bool silent;
    string group;
    string tag;
    string AppName;
}

class NotificationAction {
    string id;
    string title;
    string arguments;
}

// The pre-split free function, verbatim surface: prints the canonical
// "NOTIFY: <title> | <body>" parity line every backend matches on, then
// dispatches through the OS gate to the internal backend's bridge. The
// pre-split try/catch is preserved (the bridge never throws on any backend,
// but the defensive shape stays exactly as pre-split). On a non-Windows
// backend only the parity line prints.
public void show(Notification note) {
    // Canonical parity line printed by every backend (silverc-rs, silverc
    // self-hosted, silveri interpreter) so tests can match on it.
    printline("NOTIFY: " + note.title + " | " + note.body);
    if (osType() == OSTypes.WINDOWS) {
        // String FIELD reads cross the module boundary as locals-first
        // values (title/body captured into locals before the call -- the
        // probed-safe cross-module shape; rs stringifies field pointers in
        // larger expression positions). AppName flows to the bridge as the
        // AppUserModelId for the Action Center routing.
        string t = note.title;
        string b = note.body;
        string app = note.AppName;
        try {
            notifWinShow(t, b, app);
        } catch (exception ex) { }
    }
}
